{% extends 'layout.twig' %}
{% block title %}How Birthday Stars works{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article class="card prose">
  <h1>How Birthday Stars works</h1>
  <p><strong>A light year</strong> is how far light travels in one year: about 5.9 trillion miles. When we look at a star 10 light years away, we see light that left it 10 years ago.</p>
  <p>Birthday Stars works out how many years old you are, then finds the star with a name whose distance in light years is closest to that number. The light from that star started its trip around when you were born!</p>
  <p>Your birthday star can change as you get older, because light from farther stars keeps arriving.</p>
  <p>Journey Through the Universe is a NOIRLab / Gemini Observatory program that brings astronomers and engineers into Hawaiʻi Island classrooms every year.</p>
  <h2>Be a scientist, or a coder!</h2>
  <p>This site is a small computer program written in PHP. It reads a star catalog, does a little math, and draws the sky chart in your browser. Astronomers use the same kinds of skills every day at observatories on Maunakea. Ask your teacher about coding clubs, science fairs, and visiting the ʻImiloa Astronomy Center in Hilo.</p>
